CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20 --object-bits 11 --no-unwinding-assertions
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ constexpr member fn template as constant: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ sz<int> folds to a constant: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ runtime constexpr member fn template: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ WRONG sz<int>==1 \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
[temp.inst]/5 + [expr.const]: a call to a constexpr static member function
template with explicit template arguments must be usable as a constant
expression, which requires implicitly instantiating the specialization's
definition.  CBMC previously left the member function template uninstantiated
at constant-evaluation time (its body is type-checked lazily for ordinary
members), so the call could not be folded.  It now type-checks the constexpr
specialization's definition eagerly.  Assertions 1-3 prove the call folds in a
constant context and at run time; assertion 4 (a wrong value) must FAIL,
proving non-vacuity.
